// bubbler profile: spotify
//
// Sound is `pulseaudio`: the client and its CEF layer carry
// libpulse.so.0 (/opt/spotify/spotify and /opt/spotify/libcef.so both
// name it) and reach the server through $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pulse/native,
// which is what that grant binds. `pipewire` binds only pipewire-0, the
// native socket, and is for a client that speaks PipeWire itself or for
// the screen-share portal. Bare, either socket is playback only: no
// microphone, no other app's audio, and the private server pulseaudio
// runs behind refuses to load a module, with the policy drop-in
// installed; `lint` warns `audio-policy-missing` where it is not.
//
// No `talk "org.freedesktop.secrets"`: a music player's session token is
// adequately kept in its own config file in the private home, and the
// rule would let it talk to whatever process holds every password you
// have.

// `dbus` and `mpris name="spotify"` are what let the client start at all,
// which is worth saying because neither reads like a startup dependency.
// Before it draws anything the client asks the bus to own
// `org.mpris.MediaPlayer2.spotify`, and a `RequestName` that does not come
// back as the primary owner it reads as "a copy is already running": it
// calls `Raise` on that name and exits 0, having drawn nothing. Measured
// here with `BUBBLER_DBUS_LOG=1`: with a bare `dbus` the proxy filters the
// request (`policy: 0 (required 3)`) and the client is gone in a second.
// Without `dbus` at all it does not exit — it stops after its two zygotes
// and the GPU process, starts neither the network service nor a renderer,
// and maps no window ever. The name is exact, not a prefix: the app id is
// com.spotify.Client but the player name it publishes is
// org.mpris.MediaPlayer2.spotify, and a `*` here would own every player on
// the bus. Media keys and the desktop's player controls come with it.
//
// `network` is the sandbox's own network namespace, so Spotify Connect
// finds no devices: discovery is mDNS on the LAN and pasta does not
// bridge. `network "host"` restores it at the price of the host's own
// loopback services.
//
// `wayland` is the display, and the only one: the sandbox has no X
// display at all, so the client's Chromium layer takes the socket that
// grant binds.
//
// `dri` is the GPU: the client's Chromium layer initialises a GPU process
// on it — the shader caches it leaves in the private home are the
// evidence — and composites in software without it. Bare, so the sandbox
// gets the render nodes and not the card nodes. What bare `dri` binds
// anyway on the proprietary NVIDIA driver, whose EGL will not take a
// Wayland display without the primary node, `lint` notes.
//
// Bare by design. Add:
//   dbus {
//       talk "org.freedesktop.ScreenSaver"
//       talk "org.gnome.SettingsDaemon.MediaKeys"
//   }
//     — in place of the bare `dbus` node above, since one config carries
//       one `dbus` node: the power-save blocker CEF holds during
//       playback, and GNOME's media-key grab, which the client asks for
//       by that name. Both strings are in /opt/spotify/libcef.so; on any
//       other desktop nothing owns the second and the rule is inert. CEF
//       also tries `org.freedesktop.PowerManagement`, so add that too if
//       the screen blanks during playback.
//   notify
//     — the "now playing" notifications. Needs `dbus`.
//   tray
//     — the tray icon, which is libayatana-appindicator registering with
//       StatusNotifierWatcher. One rule, and the item itself is served on
//       the app's own unique name, so nothing needs `own`. Needs `dbus`.
//   portals
//     — a file chooser Spotify barely has; add it with
//       `home-share "Music"` if you play local files. Needs `dbus`.
wayland
dri
pulseaudio
network
dbus
mpris name="spotify"
command "spotify"
